##
    Exact arithmetic.

    'fraction' is a first-class numeric type written with a vertical bar: 22|7 is
    twenty-two sevenths, not a division that has already happened. Fractions are kept in
    lowest terms and are never rounded, so a third really is a third.

    This matters the first time a student is told that 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3. That is true of
    'real', and this program shows it — but it is a fact about binary floating point, not
    about arithmetic, and having both types available is what makes the difference visible.
##

global model Program

    function Main()
        Console.WriteLine("== thirds ==");
        Console.WriteLine("1|3 + 1|3 + 1|3 = " + (1|3 + 1|3 + 1|3));
        Console.WriteLine("as real:          " + (1.0 / 3.0 + 1.0 / 3.0 + 1.0 / 3.0));

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== tenths ==");
        Console.WriteLine("1|10 + 2|10 = " + (1|10 + 2|10));
        Console.WriteLine("as real:      " + (0.1 + 0.2));

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== lowest terms, always ==");
        Console.WriteLine("6|8   is " + 6|8);
        Console.WriteLine("100|4 is " + 100|4);
        Console.WriteLine("3|1   is " + 3|1);

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== a harmonic sum, exactly ==");
        Console.WriteLine("1 + 1|2 + ... + 1|8 = " + Program.Harmonic(8));
        Console.WriteLine("and as a real:        " + Program.Harmonic(8).ToReal());

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== raising to a power stays exact ==");
        Console.WriteLine("(1|2) ^ 3  is " + (1|2) ^ 3);
        Console.WriteLine("(2|3) ^ 2  is " + (2|3) ^ 2);

        ##
            A negative power inverts, and inverting is where the two part company. One third
            has no finite binary expansion, so a real can only get close to it; the fraction
            is the number itself.

            Note that one eighth would prove nothing here — it is a power of two, so a real
            holds it exactly. The gap only shows on a denominator that is not.
        ##
        Console.WriteLine("(3|1) ^ -1 is " + (3|1) ^ -1);
        Console.WriteLine("as a real:    " + 3.0 ^ -1.0);

        Console.WriteLine();
        Console.WriteLine("== an integer widens to a fraction on its own ==");
        fraction whole = 3;
        Console.WriteLine("3 as a fraction is " + whole);
        Console.WriteLine("3 + 1|2 is         " + (whole + 1|2));
    end function

    ##
        The sum of 1|1 through 1|n. Every partial sum is exact, so the answer is the true
        rational value rather than the nearest number a real could hold.

        Note 1|1 / i rather than 1|i. A fraction literal is written with numerals on both
        sides — it is one token, the way 3.14 is — so a fraction built from a variable is
        built by dividing. The division is exact, because both sides are fractions once the
        integer widens.
    ##
    fraction function Harmonic(integer n)
        fraction total = 0;

        for i = 1 to n
            total = total + 1|1 / i;
        end for

        yield total;
    end function

end model
